Semantic knowledge is hierarchical, and hyperbolic space is the natural geometric home for hierarchies \citep{nickel2017poincare, sarkar2011low}. A line of work therefore imposes it on representations at training time, from vision to vision--language and language \citep{Khrulkov_2020_CVPR, ermolov2022hyperbolic, atigh2022hyperbolic, bdeir2024fully, desai2023meru, pal2025compositional, chen2022fully, he2026helm, sinha2024learning}. These methods cite a premise, which we call \emph{latent hyperbolicity}: standard models are said to organize concepts already in a tree-like metric structure, because the Gromov $\delta$ of their features is low \citep{Khrulkov_2020_CVPR, bdeir2024fully, yang2025hyperbolic, tifrea2019poincare}. Is the premise right, and what does it license?

The raw reading is confounded. Gromov $\delta$ assigns zero to trees \citep{Gromov1987}, but three artifacts push it down without any hierarchy. High-dimensional clouds concentrate toward equidistance, which is a degenerate star tree: we call this the \emph{dimension confound}. Anisotropic spectra mimic low-dimensional behavior: the \emph{spectrum confound}. And the supremum over sampled quadruples is a one-quadruple extreme that does not converge: the \emph{statistic confound}. The three are visible in real models: GPT-2 M sits at its matched null despite a low raw reading, and classic sentence embedders share their raw reading with vision-contrastive models while carrying none of their structure. % exp18_text_nulls.csv, expR53_text_haar_p999_200.csv

We build the \emph{instrument} that reads $\delta$ correctly. It calibrates the estimator on reference geometries, expresses every reading as an \emph{excess} over a null cloud matched in dimension and covariance spectrum, ranks that excess against matched replicates, and adds a \emph{depth test} whose false-alarm rate and power are measured on real clouds. With it we ask of geometry what convergence work asks of similarity \citep{huh2024platonic, groger2026aristotelian}: what survives calibration, and is it shared? They calibrate similarity across models; we calibrate geometry within a model.

\textbf{The answer has three parts.} On image features the excess sits within null noise in most cells and is a fraction of the null where it survives, so the premise does not survive calibration as stated. On class centroids, clustered structure is genuine in 49 of 72 cells, but a star already produces it. Hierarchy above the superclasses is certified in 4 of 12 ImageNet backbones by a test that never fires on real clouds with randomized hubs, and a backbone trained in hyperbolic space shows the same clustering and no detected depth. The trees are moderately shared: the naive comparison manufactures an island, and once the clustering cut is controlled a moderate gap remains, every recipe recovers the human taxonomy partially, more so with supervision, and the self-supervised structure lives in angles. % expR62_samplelevel_record.csv, expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR63_meru_record.csv, exp23_treemap_controls.npz

\textbf{Our contributions are:}
\begin{enumerate}[topsep=0pt,itemsep=1pt,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{The instrument.} Estimator calibration, spectrum-matched nulls read as corrected ranks, and a depth test with measured false-alarm rate and power.
\item \textbf{The census.} Clustered structure in most models, hierarchy certified in a few, and a hyperbolic backbone as the control for imposing the geometry.
\item \textbf{The map of trees.} The island is an artifact of the cut; sharing is graded and supervision-dependent, and the self-supervised tree is angular.
\item \textbf{Consequences for practice.} What to measure before imposing curvature, and which zero-cost readout collects what is there.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Related Work and Background}
\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{The premise.} Hyperbolic representation learning builds on tree embeddability and Poincar\'{e} embeddings and now spans vision, vision--language, graphs and language \citep{sarkar2011low, nickel2017poincare, ganea2018hyperbolic, chami2019hyperbolic}. Its stated premise rests on a low raw reading for CNN and ViT features, token embeddings and word vectors, taken at the sample level and uncalibrated \citep{Khrulkov_2020_CVPR, bdeir2024fully, yang2025hyperbolic, tifrea2019poincare}.

\paragraph{Calibrating geometry against nulls.} Network science reads Gromov $\delta$ on real graphs against reference graphs: the curvature of communication and social networks is compared with that of lattices, hyperbolic grids and Erd\H{o}s--R\'{e}nyi random graphs of comparable size, and $\delta$ is read relative to the graph's diameter \citep{narayan2011curvature, kennedy2013hyperbolicity}. \citet{adcock2013treelike} pair $\delta$ with tree decompositions and find the bare value of $\delta$ too coarse a guide to tree-likeness on its own. Representation similarity, intrinsic dimension and neural collapse characterize Euclidean structure without testing tree-likeness \citep{kornblith2019cka, ansuini2019intrinsic, pope2021intrinsic, papyan2020prevalence}. The Platonic Representation Hypothesis argues that models converge toward shared representations; \citet{groger2026aristotelian} show that permutation-calibrated similarity removes most of that convergence while local structure survives, and \citet{park2024geometry} read concept hierarchy in language models from orthogonality \citep{huh2024platonic, koepke2026cave}. What is new here is the instrument for embedding clouds rather than graphs: spectrum-matched nulls at the resolution of the null replicate, a depth test against matched stars measured on real clouds, a hyperbolic backbone as the control for imposing the geometry, and a calibration of the tree-to-tree comparison itself.

\paragraph{Background.} For four points $w,x,y,z$ of a metric space, sort the three sums of distances over the pairings, $d(w,x){+}d(y,z)$, $d(w,y){+}d(x,z)$, $d(w,z){+}d(x,y)$ as $S_{\max}\ge S_{\mathrm{mid}}\ge S_{\min}$; the four-point defect is $(S_{\max}-S_{\mathrm{mid}})/2$ and $\delta$ is its supremum. Trees have $\delta=0$, and under this definition the hyperbolic plane has $\delta=\ln 2\approx0.693$, distinct from the thin-triangles constant $\ln(1+\sqrt2)\approx0.881$ \citep{nica2016strong}. We use the normalized $\delta_{\text{norm}}=\delta_{\max}/\mathrm{diam}$ throughout, on which trees $<$ hyperbolic $<$ spherical for hyperbolic regions of radius four and above (Table~\ref{tab:q10-calibration}). Sampled-quadruple estimation has known bias and scale caveats, which the calibration-plus-null design addresses, and Ollivier--Ricci curvature on a kNN graph is the local companion \citep{fournier2015computing, ollivier2009ricci}.

\section{The Instrument}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig_overview.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The raw reading cannot be read on its own.} (a) Each ImageNet backbone's raw reading (filled) and what a structureless cloud of the same dimension and spectrum reads (hollow); the nulls drift down with the dimension, and the segment between the two is the excess. (b) Two cells with the same raw reading and opposite verdicts: on ViT-T image features the null sits at the reading, on SigLIP-B centroids far above it. % exp1_delta_controls.csv, expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, expR62_samplelevel_record.csv
}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure}
\label{sec:instrument}

If the premise were right, a low raw reading would be evidence on its own. The dimension confound says it is not, so this section builds the measurement layer in whose units every later claim is stated.

\paragraph{The premise lives at the sample level; hierarchy lives at the class level.} The premise is read on the per-image features of frozen models, where the literature measures it. Hierarchy is measured on class centroids: in vision, the mean of each class's cached training embeddings, a hundred per class on ImageNet; in text, the embeddings of ``a photo of a \{class\}'' over the ImageNet classes. A \emph{cell} is one pair of model and class set; the census covers twelve vision backbones on six datasets and sixteen text models. The raw reading is the 99.9th-percentile four-point statistic $\hat\delta_{99.9}$ over half a million sampled quadruples per seed, averaged over ten seeds; the supremum, the pre-specified statistic, is reported in the appendix for the reason given below.

\paragraph{A low raw reading is not evidence.} Calibrated on reference geometries, the estimator recovers the tree zero and the $\ln 2$ constant of $\mathbb{H}^2$, and spheres stay high. Iid Gaussians, which have no structure at all, drift lower as the dimension grows (Table~\ref{tab:q10-calibration}). Figure~\ref{fig:overview}a shows the same drift on the twelve ImageNet backbones: the null of each moves with its dimension, so the gap, not the level, is the reading. That drift is the dimension confound in its purest form: the raw reading of a real backbone must be compared with the reading of a structureless cloud of the same shape.

\paragraph{The reading is an excess over a matched null.} For every cell we synthesize null clouds with the same $n$, $d$, mean and covariance spectrum. We use the Haar construction throughout: random orthogonal coefficients recombined with the real singular values, so the sample spectrum is exact. In plain terms, the spectrum null is a cloud with the same shape as the real one and no structure inside it; the hub null of Section~\ref{sec:form} keeps every cluster of the real cloud and only rearranges where the clusters sit. The \textbf{excess} is the raw reading minus the mean reading of 200 such replicates. The primary evidence is the \emph{percentile rank}, the number of replicates above the real value, and its left-tail $p$. A cell is \emph{genuine} when its Benjamini--Hochberg-corrected $p$ across the census is at most five per cent; we use the word in this sense only. A negative genuine excess certifies \emph{clustered structure}; the word ``tree-like'' is reserved for the depth test of Section~\ref{sec:form}. A positive excess means less tree-like than chance given the spectrum (Appendix~\ref{app:nulls}). Figure~\ref{fig:overview}b shows two cells with the same raw reading and opposite verdicts.

\paragraph{Two choices changed after pre-specification.} The supremum statistic did not converge under the quadruple budget on ImageNet and disagreed with the 99.9th percentile of the same defects in both directions. The Gaussian-coefficient null does not reproduce the sample spectrum it claims to match when $n$ is small relative to $d$, and it inflates the excess of low-rank clouds; the Haar null does reproduce it, so we use the Haar null and report both. Rank-based calibration is what gives permutation scores their finite-sample validity, so a $z$-score is kept only as a secondary summary, and the null is aggregation-aware in the sense of \citet[\S5]{groger2026aristotelian}: the same percentile of the same sampled defects is evaluated on every replicate. The excess is budget-stable under the record: across quadruple budgets the ImageNet excess drifts by less than half its own spread and never changes sign (Tables~\ref{tab:q1-census} and \ref{tab:q8-robust}). % expR72_budget_record_summary.csv

\paragraph{Projection and tasks.} A parameter-free map centers the features, scales the 95th-percentile norm to radius $1/\sqrt2$ and preserves directions, so Euclidean, Poincar\'{e} and cosine readouts can be compared on the intact representation; nearest-centroid and 5-way 5-shot classification score them for Section~\ref{sec:practice} (Appendix~\ref{app:corollary}). With the instrument in place, we read the premise where it is read. % exp1, exp2

\section{Findings I: Latent Hyperbolicity, Calibrated}
\label{sec:form}

