If standard models were latently hyperbolic, the instrument should find an excess on their image features, where the premise is read. It finds one within null noise in most cells, and what survives lives on class centroids, where a star already passes the census.

\paragraph{The premise does not survive calibration.} We read it first on per-image features, roughly a thousand stratified training images per cell for CIFAR-100 and DTD, where the raw reading lies in the low band the literature reports. Calibrated, it is not genuine in most cells, and where it is genuine it removes between {{SL_FRAC_LO}} and {{SL_FRAC_HI}} per cent of the null reading, the larger figure for DINOv2-G on CIFAR-100 images, and it is largest in the DINOv2 family, whose structure is the angular tree of Section~\ref{sec:content}, which cosine collects. This is the reading that motivates hyperbolic methods, and calibrated it is what a random cloud of the same dimension and spectrum produces, up to a fraction of the null (Table~\ref{tab:q3-sample}). % expR62_samplelevel_record.csv

\paragraph{Clustered structure is the rule.} Figure~\ref{fig:excess} and Table~\ref{tab:census} give the census of record cell by cell. Almost every cell is below its matched null, and in 49 of the 72 the gap is one that chance does not produce. On ImageNet and CIFAR-100, the two sets with a real hierarchy, the count is 18 of 24. The structure belongs to no single family or architecture: every family contributes genuine cells and two self-supervised ResNets read the same as the ViTs. What moves the reading is the label set: the smallest backbone is genuine on one dataset only, the flat datasets sit at the resolution limit of ten classes, and a class-count control shows that the reading follows the hierarchy of the labels rather than their number (Table~\ref{tab:q8-robust}). % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, expR59_imagenet_bootstrap_summary.csv, expR45_convnet_rows.csv, expR60_c_sweep_record.csv

\paragraph{The count survives resampling.} The census null accounts for replicate variability but not for image resampling or the estimator's seeds. Folding all three into one spread, or repeating the census on thirty resampled centroid sets, leaves most of the count in place, and the cells that move are on the transfer sets (Table~\ref{tab:census}). % expR66_joint_sensitivity_summary.csv

\paragraph{A star passes the census.} The excess measures structure beyond second moments, and a pure star already has such structure. A synthetic star of thirty clusters with no hierarchy scores an excess as deep as any real cell. We call this the \emph{star caveat}. The excess therefore measures how classes are arranged, not the geometry they are embedded in, and a negative excess says clustered, not deep. Whether the arrangement is deeper than a star is a separate question, which needs a test of its own (Table~\ref{tab:q10-calibration}). % expR42_star_calibration.csv

\paragraph{Depth is certified on ImageNet and not on CIFAR-100.} The depth test compares the real centroids with a matched star: Gaussian hubs at the real hub radius and, within each cluster, a Haar sample with the cluster's own covariance, both read under a null that randomizes the hubs and keeps every cluster intact. Because the null keeps the clusters, the test certifies hierarchy above the labelled clusters only. On ImageNet the real cloud is more hierarchical than its matched star in {{N_IN}} of 12 backbones, three supervised ViTs and DINOv2-L, and never less. The other eight are not detected, which is not the same as not hierarchical. For the four certified backbones the same clusters with random hubs raise no alarm in any zero-strength run, so the verdict comes from their real hub arrangement, which the test reads as deeper than the implanted two-level tree. The certified set shifts with the choice of frame, as Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth} shows. CIFAR-100, with clusters of a few points, lies outside the validated regime. An earlier isotropic star produced false alarms and is withdrawn (Figure~\ref{fig:depth}a). % expR56_depth_variants.csv, expR55b_depth_power_leafframe.csv, expR64b_wn30.csv

\paragraph{A spectrum-matched star confirms the four.} We repeated the test with the star's hubs drawn as a Haar resample of the real hubs, so that the star carries the exact hub spectrum. The certified set is the same four, with $z$ from ${{HAAR_Z_HI}}$ to ${{HAAR_Z_LO}}$. On the real clouds with implanted trees the new star raises {{FA_HAAR}} false alarms at zero strength and detects {{POWER_HAAR}} at full strength. Read instead as a rank over the ten star seeds, at a resolution of one part in eleven, the verdict places the real cloud below every star seed for the certified four and for most of the others alike, so the rank does not separate certified from uncertified backbones while $z$ does. Supervision on leaf labels alone can produce this depth and need not: of two ViT-B trained on ImageNet-1k leaf labels, the augreg recipe is certified under both stars and DeiT-B is not, so the IN-21k label hierarchy is not what makes the census ViTs deep (Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth}). % expR69_depth_haarhubs_summary.csv, expR70_inet1k_supervised.csv

\paragraph{On real clouds the test is conservative and weak.} We measured the test on the real ImageNet clouds themselves, replacing the hub arrangement by an implanted two-level tree of graded strength while keeping every within-cluster offset, and the census cannot tell the implanted tree from the star. The test raises no false alarm, declaring none of the sixty zero-strength runs hierarchical. A clean implanted tree is detected in {{R9B_NDET_PHRASE}}, and the misses are a matter of noise level: the within-cluster spread of the real clouds is between one and four times their between-hub spread, the synthetic sweep covered ratios below one, and once the real offsets are shrunk into that range every backbone is detected, and two contrastive backbones also fire at zero strength, so the shrunk variant is a diagnostic of power, not a substitute test. The test is therefore conservative and weak at ImageNet's noise level: its positive verdicts stand, and a backbone it does not certify is not thereby flat. A trained control, a ViT-B fine-tuned with a hierarchical objective, is inconclusive (Table~\ref{tab:q5-power}, Figure~\ref{fig:depth}b). % expR64b_wn30.csv, expR64b_wn30_summary.csv, expR65_hier_finetune.csv

