\paragraph{Imposing the geometry does not create detected depth.} MERU embeds images and text on the Lorentz hyperboloid with an entailment objective, and its released Euclidean twin is a CLIP baseline with the same backbone, data and recipe minus the hyperbolic lift, so the pair isolates the geometry \citep{desai2023meru}. Its embeddings sit in the near-flat regime of the hyperboloid, at a Lorentz-to-Euclidean distance ratio of ${{MERU_RATIO}}$, so the control tests the training objective rather than a curved geometry. Passed through the instrument unchanged, the two read the same excess on ImageNet images, and MERU's reading in its own Lorentz metric equals its Euclidean reading to the fourth decimal. Under the depth test, conservative and weak at this noise level, neither model is detected as more hierarchical than a matched star, and MERU is never ahead of its twin. Training in hyperbolic space therefore neither creates the clustering nor adds detected depth (Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth}). % expR63_meru_record.csv, expR71_meru_radii.csv

\paragraph{Neural collapse is the flat limit, not the reading.} Neural collapse predicts that the class means of a supervised classifier converge to a simplex equiangular tight frame \citep{papyan2020prevalence}. An exact frame is the extreme case of the star caveat, and our reading differs from it in two ways. The arrangement we certify is not flat, because class means cluster around superclass hubs that a frame would erase, and cutting the centroid dendrograms recovers the CIFAR-100 superclasses. The same clustered structure also appears without class labels, on transfer datasets and in causal language models, outside the setting where neural collapse is stated, and the depth test asks what neural collapse does not, whether there is hierarchy above the hubs (Table~\ref{tab:q11-interventions}). % night/orc_bridges.csv

\paragraph{Scale deepens the excess for one family only.} Figure~\ref{fig:excess} shows that scale does not deepen the excess in general, with one exception: down the DINOv2 rows the excess deepens with size on CIFAR-10 and, weakly, on CIFAR-100. Training moves the raw reading within an architecture: fine-tuning on a task with no class hierarchy raises it, it falls across transformer depth, and shuffling the embedding-to-label correspondence leaves it unchanged (Table~\ref{tab:q11-interventions}). What survives at the class level is clustered structure, genuine in most cells, rarely deep, and not created by the embedding geometry. Whether that structure is shared across models, and whose tree it is, is the question we turn to next. % expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, analysis4_finetuning.csv, e1_delta_by_layer.csv


\section{Findings II: Whose Tree}
\label{sec:content}

If the measured trees were a shared representation of reality, models should agree with each other and with human taxonomy. Whether they appear to depends on how the trees are measured.

\paragraph{The naive map manufactures an island.} We extract each model's hierarchy as a dendrogram over the shared centroids and compare hierarchies by the adjusted Rand index between cut partitions and by the cophenetic correlation between tree and distances. Under the naive configuration, Euclidean distances with average linkage, the map is spectacular: supervised ViTs and contrastive VLMs form one block, and the label-free DINOv2 family appears as an island that agrees with no supervised model, which we call the \emph{island}. Its cuts are degenerate: DINOv2's Euclidean dendrograms chain, almost every class falls into one cluster, and the index is forced to zero by construction. A configuration must therefore be selected by a criterion fixed without reference to the agreements being compared: we require the highest cophenetic fidelity among configurations whose reference cuts are non-degenerate for every model, which selects cosine-average on ImageNet and cosine-complete on CIFAR-100. The criterion is applied per dataset, because degeneracy is a property of the dataset, and it never sees the agreements it selects for. Under every non-degenerate configuration the gap stays moderate and never returns to the island. Figure~\ref{fig:treemap} shows the map before and after this control (Table~\ref{tab:q6-treemap}). % exp22_tree_similarity_{imagenet,cifar100}.npz, exp23_treemap_controls.npz, exp23_config_diagnostics.json

\paragraph{The categorical island is an artifact, and a moderate gap remains.} Under the selected configurations three measures agree. At the cut, the island shrinks from categorical to moderate: the DINOv2 family agrees with the block at {{ARI_BIG}} against {{ARI_BLOCK}} within the block, and under the other admissible configurations about a third of the within-block agreement is missing. In cophenetic correlation a gap remains under every configuration, and in triplet agreement, which needs no cut, it closes under cosine-average and persists under the other configurations, the Euclidean ones included. Across every non-degenerate configuration, label-free models recover the human taxonomy partially and supervision deepens the resemblance on average across the supervised backbones tested: paired within each admissible configuration, the label-supervised ViT recovers more than each DINOv2 model in {{PAIRED_PHRASE}} (Table~\ref{tab:q6-treemap}). % exp23_treemap_controls.npz, expR58_treemap_cutfree_summary.csv, exp28_recovery_per_config.csv

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.6\linewidth]{figures/fig_treemap_controls.pdf}
\caption{\textbf{The categorical island is a chaining artifact; about a third of the within-block agreement is missing under most configurations.} Plotted: pairwise ARI between the dendrogram cuts of the 12 vision models on ImageNet under the naive configuration (a) and the selected one (b), with the mean DINOv2-vs-block ARI above each panel. % exp22, exp23
}
\label{fig:treemap}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{The self-supervised tree lives in angles.} DINOv2's semantics concentrate in the angular component of its representation: for DINOv2-L, sibling triplets are resolved far better under cosine than under Euclidean distance. We call this structure the \emph{angular tree}. It is why DINOv2's Euclidean dendrograms chain into the island and why the triplet gap closes only under cosine-average. The census agrees when we repeat it on cosine geometry, with L2-normalized centroids, geodesic distances and the same Haar null on the normalized cloud: the verdict matches the Euclidean census in {{AGREE}} of 72 cells, and DINOv2 on ImageNet is genuine under both readings. We report the cosine census as robustness, never as the record (Table~\ref{tab:q1-census}). % exp10_local_vs_global.csv, expR57_census_cosine_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{WordNet alignment follows supervision and recipe, not tree-likeness.} The correlation between inter-centroid distances and WordNet tree distances on ImageNet orders the families by supervision: highest for the contrastive VLMs, then the supervised ViTs, and lowest for DINOv2, with shuffle and Gaussian controls at zero. Leaf-label supervision does not guarantee the alignment: the augreg ViT-B trained on ImageNet-1k is aligned like the IN-21k ViTs, while DeiT-B is nearly unaligned yet recovers the CIFAR-100 superclasses as well as any backbone, so alignment is recipe-dependent even among leaf-supervised ViTs. DINOv2 is nevertheless genuine on ImageNet and both CIFAR sets and recovers the true CIFAR-100 superclasses from its dendrograms, against zero for permuted labels. Across the three families, WordNet content and robustness of the excess anti-correlate, as Table~\ref{tab:census} shows: the supervised and contrastive families are the most aligned and the most fragile, DINOv2 the least aligned and the most robust. Alignment with the human taxonomy is therefore something training puts in, not something tree-likeness reveals. On DBpedia Classes, a three-level ontology independent of WordNet, no configuration degenerates, and the three text embedders recover the true level-2 partition in every configuration and agree with each other. Their excess there is genuine with every replicate above the real value, so the recovered hierarchy is real, shared and configuration-robust (Table~\ref{tab:q7-wordnet}). % exp3_alignment.csv, expR70_inet1k_supervised.csv, exp8_p1_recovery.csv, exp27_dbpedia_treemap.json, expR61_dbpedia_record.csv

\paragraph{The recovery is not WordNet circularity.} ImageNet classes are WordNet synsets, so recovering WordNet there could be circular. Three controls bound the circularity: WordNet-coherent groupings read a lower raw $\delta$ than random ones in {{GRP_PHRASE}}, single-image distances still correlate with WordNet, and the decoupling of alignment from robustness is the opposite of what label injection would produce (Table~\ref{tab:q7-wordnet}). % exp1, exp8_p6_pooling.csv, exp27_dbpedia_treemap.json

\paragraph{In text, clustered structure depends on recipe, scale and probe.} On the thousand ImageNet class names the sentence embedders are not genuine, despite raw readings indistinguishable from vision backbones whose excess is. Within causal language models on the same probe, GPT-2 L and XL are genuine under every construction of the null and statistic and under cosine, GPT-2 S is genuine at the margin under the record and flips under the supremum and under cosine, and GPT-2 M is not genuine under the record: robust at the larger scales, fragile at the smaller. Pythia and OLMo-1B are genuine at every scale tested, and extraction protocol and prompt template move the fragile values but not the scale ordering (Table~\ref{tab:q2-text}). % expR53_text_haar_p999_200.csv, expR53_text_haar_sup_200.csv, expR53_text_gauss_p999_200.csv, expR48b_text_census200_bs1.csv, expR57_text_cosine_haar_p999_200.csv

\paragraph{Local agreement survives calibration.} We apply the permutation calibration of \citet{groger2026aristotelian} to the sixty-six model pairs, and mutual-kNN agreement and centroid CKA both survive with every pair significant, while the Poincar\'{e} reading improves neither (Table~\ref{tab:q13-local}). What a practitioner can collect from this local agreement is the subject of the next section. Centroids distill a relational base that models share locally, in who is near whom, even where their trees differ. % exp21b_local_global_K200.csv

\section{Consequences for Imposing Curvature}
\label{sec:practice}

If the raw reading were evidence, a practitioner could set the curvature from it. It is not, so this section states what to read before imposing curvature and which readout collects the structure that survives.

\paragraph{The raw reading cannot select a curvature.} \citet{Khrulkov_2020_CVPR} set the curvature of the Poincar\'{e} ball from the measured relative hyperbolicity as $c=(0.144/\delta_{\text{rel}})^2$ with $\delta_{\text{rel}}=2\delta/\mathrm{diam}$. Since the raw reading is dominated by the dimension and spectrum confounds, such a curvature tracks the backbone's width rather than its hierarchy. Structureless Gaussian clouds would receive a curvature from ${{C_LO}}$ at $d{=}{{D_LO}}$ to ${{C_HI}}$ at $d{=}{{D_HI}}$. The excess is the quantity to read instead: on ImageNet centroids it removes {{IN_FRAC_LO}} to {{IN_FRAC_HI}} per cent of the null reading, many times the null's own spread, and on image features it sits within noise in most cells (Table~\ref{tab:census}). % exp1_delta_controls.csv, expR52_census_haar_p999_200.csv, expR62_samplelevel_record.csv

\paragraph{The calibrated reading predicts the zero-cost gain.} Within each hierarchical dataset, the record excess predicts the nearest-centroid gain of the Poincar\'{e} readout on all four datasets and the few-shot gain on three, with confidence intervals that exclude zero and a family control that leaves the within-dataset relation in place. The depth verdict predicts no gain anywhere. The prediction is a diagnostic read off the families it is scored on, not a policy, and cosine remains the safe default (Table~\ref{tab:q9-corollary}, Figure~\ref{fig:bestmetric}). % expR68_corollary_excess.csv

\paragraph{Measure before imposing.} For a frozen backbone the instrument answers three questions in minutes. Is there structure beyond a spectrum-matched null, and is it deeper than a star? The excess and its rank answer the first, and the depth test the second, conservative and weak at ImageNet's noise level, so that a positive verdict certifies and a negative one says nothing. Where the structure lives, in angles or in distances, the cosine reading of Section~\ref{sec:content} says.

\paragraph{A zero-cost readout collects what is there.} Changing the readout metric costs nothing, and cosine collects the angular tree. The Poincar\'{e} readout adds from ${{GAIN_LO}}$ to ${{GAIN_HI}}$ pp over cosine for the contrastive VLMs on the transfer sets, while for the other families its effect is inconsistent in sign. On flat-label datasets no metric helps, and sample-level tasks never benefit (Table~\ref{tab:q9-corollary}). % exp2_metric_controls.csv, exp2b_normalized_stack.csv, exp24_val_metric_selection.csv

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What the paper establishes.} Read correctly, foundation models organize classes into clustered structure that is occasionally hierarchical and moderately shared; they do not converge to one common tree, and their raw tree-likeness is not evidence for hyperbolic geometry. In three acts: the premise of latent hyperbolicity is confounded and its sample-level reading sits within null noise; what survives at the class level is clustered structure in 49 of 72 cells, hierarchy above the superclasses in 4 of 12 ImageNet backbones, no detected depth from imposing the geometry, and a moderately shared, supervision-dependent recovery of the human taxonomy, angular for the self-supervised family; and what follows is that curvature should be measured rather than assumed.

\paragraph{Open questions.} Why the structure is clustered but rarely certified as deep, and why the self-supervised tree is angular, are open: every recipe recovers the superclass hubs, yet only four backbones place them detectably beyond a star.

\paragraph{Limitations.} (i)~The excess certifies structure beyond second moments, star-like clustering included; the depth test certifies hierarchy above its frame only, is conservative and weak at ImageNet's noise level, leaves hierarchy within the clusters untested, and its one trained positive control is inconclusive (Table~\ref{tab:q5-power}). (ii)~The census covers class-centroid geometry only. (iii)~Causal-LM values, not their scale ordering, are template-conditioned. (iv)~The objective--geometry link is correlational: it rests chiefly on DINOv2's scale range, and two leaf-label ViTs show that supervision can produce depth and alignment without guaranteeing either, as Table~\ref{tab:q4-depth} reports. (v)~Downstream scope is prototype-based metric tasks with modest gains. (vi)~The descriptor $\xi$ tracks norm dispersion rather than curvature, as Table~\ref{tab:q12-xi} reports. (vii)~A causal LM's class representation depends on the extraction protocol as much as on the model. (viii)~The sample-level reading covers two datasets and one image budget.

