\subsection{Is the clustered structure genuine? The vision census under every construction}
\label{app:census}
\label{app:nulls}
\paragraph{What each null preserves.} The spectrum-matched null, Gaussian or Haar, preserves $n$, $d$, the mean and the covariance spectrum and destroys all higher-order structure; the PC-permutation null preserves each principal component's marginal and destroys the dependence across components; the label shuffle keeps the point cloud itself and destroys the embedding--label correspondence; and the hub-randomizing null keeps each cluster's geometry and hub assignment and destroys the hub arrangement, which is why it serves the depth test only when read against a matched star. ``Genuine'' in the text always means beyond the first of these. In plain terms, the spectrum null is a cloud with the same shape as the real one and no structure inside it; the hub null of Section~\ref{sec:form} keeps every cluster of the real cloud and only rearranges where the clusters sit.
