\paragraph{Hierarchy depth, not class count.} Because $C$ sets the quadruple population, we sweep ImageNet subsets $C\in\{10,\dots,1000\}$ in two compositions: random (spanning the full hierarchy) and WordNet-coherent (siblings, effectively \emph{flat}). At fixed $C$, random subsets are more exploitable than coherent ones and carry a larger excess at small $C$ (CLIP-L at every $C$, DINOv2-L up to $C{=}100$, DINOv2-G up to $C{=}20$; beyond that the two compositions converge). Random subsets still score {{C10_LO}} to {{C10_HI}} at $C{=}10$, so MNIST's flat verdict is not a class-count artifact, and DINOv2-G stays below its null at every $C$ (excess {{DV2G_C1000}} at $C{=}1000$, $r{=}200$), where the supremum had placed it at the null. % expR60_c_sweep_record.csv, exp19_c_sweep.csv
